  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:34:49.022Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSection 51 of the NIRC requires individuals to file an income tax return, with specific deadlines based on their income sources. Generally, returns must be filed by April 15 of each year for the preceding taxable year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe controlling authority for this requirement is Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51 outlines the obligations for individuals to file income tax returns. It specifies that every Filipino citizen residing in the Philippines, as well as those residing abroad on income sourced from the Philippines, must file a return. The section also details the filing deadlines, indicating that individual taxpayers must submit their returns by the 15th day of April each year, covering income from the previous year. Additionally, individuals subject to capital gains tax must file returns within 30 days after each transaction.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nIn practice, this means that individuals must be diligent in tracking their income and ensuring timely filing of their tax returns to avoid penalties. The April 15 deadline is crucial for compliance, and individuals engaged in capital transactions must be aware of their additional filing obligations. Failure to comply with these requirements can result in fines or other legal consequences.",
